---
description: Isolate external data providers from SafeEdge domain code
alwaysApply: true
---

# SafeEdge — External integrations

SafeEdge may ingest data from multiple external providers. Each provider is a source system, not the domain model.

Assume external integrations are unstable unless they are explicitly backed by a supported public contract.

Possible providers include a current-odds bookmaker frontend API, a commercial odds API, a football statistics API, a historical odds dataset, or a results provider. Do not assume they expose the same capabilities.

## Required boundary

```text
External provider
      ↓
Provider-specific client
      ↓
Provider-specific DTOs
      ↓
Provider-specific mapper / normalizer
      ↓
SafeEdge internal domain
```

Example: Tippmix is one possible current-odds provider. It may be an unofficial/internal frontend API, so treat it as unstable and isolate it behind an adapter. Tippmix DTOs do not define the SafeEdge domain.

## Isolation

- Provider DTOs, field names, payload shapes, IDs, and client types stay inside that provider's integration area (for example `tippmix/` or `statsapi/`).
- Internal domain code must not depend on provider response classes.
- Persistence entities must not depend directly on provider DTOs.
- Candidate analysis, scoring, settlement, and backtests must operate on SafeEdge internal models, not external response structures.
- Provider field names, market names, source IDs, and quirks must not leak through Managers, engines, API DTOs, or the frontend.
- Parsing, coercion, null-handling, and normalization belong in the provider adapter.

HTTP and other outbound I/O for a provider live only in that provider's client. Domain engines and repositories must not perform outbound provider calls. Do not scatter URL fragments, headers, cookies, or parsing details across the codebase.

Do not generate or implement a provider client unless the current task asks for it.

## Capabilities

Do not introduce one universal interface that assumes every provider can do everything.

Different providers may support different capabilities, for example current events, current odds, historical odds, results, competition metadata, statistics, lineups, or injuries.

Prefer capability-oriented abstractions (`OddsProvider`, `ResultsProvider`, `HistoricalOddsProvider`, `StatisticsProvider`, and similar) only when a real implementation needs them. Do not create those types speculatively.

## Internal identity

SafeEdge should eventually own its own identifiers. Provider IDs are external references, not internal primary identity.

```text
SafeEdgeEventId
    ↕
ProviderEventReference(provider, externalId)
```

The same idea applies to competitions, markets, selections, and teams when needed. Do not design a complete identity-resolution system until a task requires it.

## Market normalization

Provider-specific market representation must be normalized before betting logic uses it.

External sources may represent the same idea (`+1.5` handicap, Asian Handicap `+1.0` / `+0.75`, double chance, over/under) with different IDs, names, codes, nested fields, or line values.

SafeEdge betting logic must not identify markets through arbitrary provider display strings scattered through the application. Normalization belongs at the integration/domain boundary.

## Time and odds observations

External timestamps must be normalized into SafeEdge's UTC-based time model.

Odds received from a provider are observations at a particular time. They are not timeless mutable properties. See `betting-domain.mdc`.

## Payload preservation

Preserving a raw provider response is allowed when useful for diagnostics, auditing, debugging, mapper failures, or reprocessing.

Raw payloads are evidence, not a substitute for a normalized domain model. Downstream logic must read internal types, not JSON blobs.

## Failure handling

- Distinguish transport/connectivity errors from invalid provider data.
- Validate required response structure before normalization.
- Log enough provider context to diagnose ingestion failures.
- Never silently invent missing values.
- Never substitute a different market or selection because provider data is malformed.
- Tolerate additive unknown fields where practical.
- Integration failures must not silently corrupt or partially overwrite previously stored historical data.

## Historical file sources

Historical source DTOs (football-data.co.uk column names, CSV rows) stay inside that provider's adapter. Do not leak them into engines, CandidateEngine, BacktestEngine, or strategy domain.

football-data.co.uk Asian Handicap lines are home-team perspective; SafeEdge `BettingMarket.line` is the same home-side convention. Conversion must be explicitly tested. Do not silently invert.

Missing historical odds or lines are never fabricated, interpolated, or filled from 1X2. Incomplete quotes skip that quote only.

Static historical CSV imports are idempotent upserts of source records. They are not live Tippmix `odds_snapshot` ticks and must never be labelled as Tippmix odds.

Do not invent `kickoffUtc` or `observedAt` when the source has only a date or no timestamp. Fake times must not be used as look-ahead protection.

Historical bulk imports stay sequential and reuse the single-season importer. One source-file failure must not abort the remaining league-season files. Re-runs are idempotent upserts, not append-only snapshots.

Historical feature generation consumes internal match facts only. Provider CSV columns and bookmaker odds must not leak into pre-match football-performance features.

## When uncertain

If a provider field cannot be mapped cleanly to the internal model, stop and ask instead of leaking the source shape inward.
